window.LATTICE_GUIDE_INDEX = [{"href":"index.html","title":"Lattice User Guide","text":"Lattice is a writing app for LaTeX and Typst papers on the Mac. Your paper stays an ordinary folder on disk; Lattice adds the PDF beside it, your papers and citations, version history, Overleaf sync and an AI agent. This guide covers the main tasks, one page each, for Lattice on the Mac. Lattice ships often, so the guide is updated with every release; Help › What's New in Lattice lists what changed in the version you run. Lattice's own menu opens it from Help › User Guide. 中文版：Lattice 使用指南。"},{"href":"index.html#getting-started","title":"Lattice User Guide › Getting started","text":"Getting started: install Lattice and TeX, open or create a project, the Get started checklist. Panels and workspaces: arrange panels, save layouts, rename or delete a workspace."},{"href":"index.html#writing","title":"Lattice User Guide › Writing","text":"Writing: the editor, Source and Visual, outlines, code files and notes. Boards: whiteboards in your project. History and Compare versions: undo any change, save versions, compare two of them, track changes."},{"href":"index.html#building-and-pdf","title":"Lattice User Guide › Building and PDF","text":"Building and fixing errors: ⌘S, the PDF beside your source, build problems, auxiliary files, exporting. Typst documents: write and build .typ files."},{"href":"index.html#citations-and-papers","title":"Lattice User Guide › Citations and Papers","text":"Citations and papers: ⌘⇧K and ⌘⇧L, the Papers library, Zotero, Check references."},{"href":"index.html#agent","title":"Lattice User Guide › Agent","text":"The Agent: Claude Code, Codex or your own model, working on your paper."},{"href":"index.html#overleaf","title":"Lattice User Guide › Overleaf","text":"Overleaf sync: open an Overleaf project, upload one, how sync behaves."},{"href":"index.html#settings","title":"Lattice User Guide › Settings","text":"Settings and shortcuts: where each setting lives, the keys worth learning. Plugins and themes: themes, code themes, install a plugin."},{"href":"index.html#troubleshooting","title":"Lattice User Guide › Troubleshooting","text":"Troubleshooting and FAQ: what to try when something goes wrong, and how to report it."},{"href":"index.html#developing-plugins","title":"Lattice User Guide › Developing plugins","text":"For people who want to write a plugin or a theme and list it in the Lattice market. Your first plugin: build a hello-world plugin, run it from its folder, read its logs. Plugin manifest and API: permissions, calling Lattice, results and actions. Publishing a plugin: reproducible packs, the registry pull request, review, signing, updates and the kill switch. Making a theme: write a lattice-theme.json, try it, publish it. Screenshots show Lattice in light mode, with the bundled tutorial paper open. Your window may differ slightly by version and theme. On Windows and Linux, Lattice is still the classic app, which looks much the same but differs in places this guide doesn't cover."},{"href":"agent.html","title":"The Agent","text":"The Agent panel runs a coding agent that reads your project and papers, fixes build errors and edits files. Every change it makes shows as a diff you can review or undo, and lands in History. Lattice includes no model or AI subscription of its own: you bring one."},{"href":"agent.html#connect-an-agent","title":"The Agent › Connect an agent","text":"Open the Agent panel (⌘⌥3). Then use one of: Claude Code or Codex on your Mac, signed in as you. Install the CLI, sign in once in Terminal (for example claude auth login), and pick it in the model menu under the message box. Another agent CLI you have installed, such as Cursor, OpenCode or Droid. Your own API: Settings › Agent › Model endpoint takes an OpenAI-compatible or Anthropic-compatible address (More protocols has others), a key (kept in your Keychain) and the Models to offer; Test connection checks it. The Agent runs it through Pi, a one-time download of about 150 MB that you install under Settings › Agent › Providers; no command-line tool is needed. Proofread, Polish and revision requests use the endpoint only once you choose one of its models as the proofreading model under Providers. The rest of Settings › Agent: Providers sets the default provider and the models Lattice uses for its own small tasks (proofreading, commit messages, compile repair). Display sets the Agent's font size, from 11 to 20 px, apart from the editors' sizes. MCP adds tools such as Zotero to the Agent; Skills manages the skills it can use; Usage & limits shows what you have used. Archived threads: see below. Environment shows the variables the Agent receives, read from your login shell and macOS's proxy settings, with Restart agent to pick up a change. Providers, MCP, Skills, Usage & limits and Archived threads need a project open."},{"href":"agent.html#work-with-it","title":"The Agent › Work with it","text":"Type what you want in the message box and press Return. Mention a file to point it there. To attach files to a message, choose + › Files and folders, or drop them from the Finder on the Agent panel. Any kind of file works, such as a Word document or a spreadsheet; the Agent gets the file, and it isn't added to your project. Polish, Revise, Proofread and Check references start common tasks. To work on one passage, select it in the editor first. In the editor, select text to Proofread (⌘⌥P), Polish or Rewrite with an instruction in place (Rewrite selection… in the command palette), with a reason for each edit. Annotate a passage (select it, choose Annotate, write what should change), then Save and revise now: the Agent proposes an edit for each annotation and you accept or reject it in place. Revise from annotations in the command palette does every open annotation in rounds. A failed build's Ask the agent sends that problem straight here. The permission menu in the panel's header decides when the Agent asks first: Never ask, Ask for risky actions, or Ask before outside edits and network. Long lines in the Agent's diffs wrap. The wrap button in a diff's toolbar, or ⌥Z, turns wrapping off and on again, and Lattice remembers the choice."},{"href":"agent.html#threads","title":"The Agent › Threads","text":"Threads are kept per project; the clock next to the thread name lists earlier ones. Settings › Agent › Archived threads lists archived threads, such as ones archived after a while unused, and can restore one to the history or delete it for good. Threads you had in the classic version show up as well. Each time the Agent starts, it adds the classic version's threads it doesn't have yet and says how many in a short notice. When you continue one, the Agent's first reply gets a condensed recap of it: the last few messages in more detail, older ones shortened, and the oldest left out of a long thread. It doesn't get the classic version's tool calls, checkpoints or session."},{"href":"agent.html#a-window-of-its-own","title":"The Agent › A window of its own","text":"Open in separate window in the Agent panel's header (or Open the Agent in a separate window in the command palette) moves the Agent into its own window, with the thread, your draft and a running reply intact. Return the Agent to the workspace in the command palette, or closing the window, puts it back. The window keeps its place and size for each project, and opens again with the project."},{"href":"boards.html","title":"Boards","text":"A board is a whiteboard saved in your project as an Excalidraw .excalidraw file, drawn natively by Lattice. Use it for figures you are still thinking through, diagrams to show co-authors, or notes beside the paper. Boards from excalidraw.com open as they are, and boards you make here open there."},{"href":"boards.html#create-a-board","title":"Boards › Create a board","text":"In the Project panel's + menu, choose New board: it creates untitled.excalidraw and opens it. You can also right-click a folder or an empty area, choose New file and give the name an .excalidraw ending, for example ideas.excalidraw. An existing .excalidraw file from elsewhere comes in through Import files… in the same menu."},{"href":"boards.html#draw","title":"Boards › Draw","text":"The toolbar at the top has the lock (keeps the current tool selected), the hand (pans the canvas), selection, rectangle, diamond, ellipse, arrow, line, draw (a freehand pen), text and eraser. More tools holds image, frame and lasso selection. Arrows snap to shapes and stay attached when you move them. Bottom left: zoom out, the zoom level, zoom in, then undo and redo. Bottom right: Help, with the keyboard shortcuts. Top left: the menu, with Find on canvas (⌘F), Export image... (⌘⇧E), Help and resetting the canvas. Export writes a PNG or SVG of the board or the selection, with or without the background, in dark mode, at a scale you choose, or copies it to the clipboard. Boards save as you draw, appear in History like any other file, and sync to Overleaf as ordinary files. The Agent can read and edit them too."},{"href":"building.html#build","title":"Building and fixing errors › Build","text":"Press ⌘S to save and build. By default Lattice also builds on its own after you stop typing; Settings › Editor & builds › Automatic build switches that to Manual only, which leaves building to ⌘S and the build button. The build button above the editor shows the result and how long it took; click it to build again, or ⇧-click it for a clean rebuild. From the command palette (⌘K): Clean rebuild deletes the build's intermediate files and builds from scratch. Try it when a build behaves strangely. Clean aux files only deletes them. Stop build stops a build that is running. The ⋯ menu above the editor has Clean rebuild and Stop the build too. With a project open, Settings › Editor & builds › Builds also has the Compile engine (pdfLaTeX, XeLaTeX or LuaLaTeX; a latexmkrc in the project overrides it), Allow shell escape, and Stop on first error (off by default, so TeX carries on past errors as Overleaf does). A LuaLaTeX project gets Live preview too, which shows your edits in the PDF as you type; it downloads its engine the first time."},{"href":"building.html#the-pdf-beside-your-source","title":"Building and fixing errors › The PDF beside your source","text":"Each build updates the PDF panel in place. To move between the two: In the PDF, double-click a sentence to jump to the source line that wrote it. In the source, press ⌘⇧J (Jump to PDF) to show the current line in the PDF. The PDF can also move to a window of its own for a second screen: Open the PDF in a separate window in the command palette (⌘K), and Return the PDF to the workspace to bring it back."},{"href":"building.html#auxiliary-files","title":"Building and fixing errors › Auxiliary files","text":"A LaTeX build leaves .aux, .log, SyncTeX and other files beside your document. The Project panel hides them either way (Show hidden files shows them). To keep them out of the folder itself, turn on Settings › Editor & builds › Hide auxiliary files. From the next build, they go to the project's hidden .research/build folder and only the PDF stays beside the document. A document that fails there, because a package ignores TeX's output directory (minted 2.x, for example), builds beside itself instead. Auxiliary files › Clean on the same page deletes them."},{"href":"building.html#read-and-fix-a-build-problem","title":"Building and fixing errors › Read and fix a build problem","text":"When a build fails, the build button turns red and a card opens over the PDF. Each problem names its file and line, shows the source around it, and says in plain words what LaTeX objected to. The card can float over the page or dock below it. Click a problem to jump to its line. F8 and ⇧F8 step through problems from the keyboard. Messages lists the problems; Log shows the raw LaTeX log, with Copy log. Ask the agent hands one problem to the Agent; Fix all asks it to fix every problem. Some problems come with a fix of their own: Install for a missing package, Switch to pdfLaTeX, or Update TeX packages."},{"href":"building.html#export","title":"Building and fixing errors › Export","text":"Export for arXiv… (project menu or File) writes one ZIP that builds on its own. Export ZIP writes the whole project, which Overleaf can import. The PDF toolbar's menu has Save PDF as…, and Export review PDF…, which writes the PDF with the annotations you pick as ordinary PDF comments."},{"href":"citations.html#cite-and-reference-while-you-type","title":"Citations and papers › Cite and reference while you type","text":"⌘⇧K Insert citation: search your bibliography by key, title or author, then press Return. Lattice inserts \\cite{key} at the cursor. ⌘⇧L Insert reference: search the labels in your project (eq:, fig:, tab: and so on) and insert \\ref{…}. Hover a \\cite key to see the reference it points to, or press ⌘⌥K with the cursor on it."},{"href":"citations.html#the-papers-library","title":"Citations and papers › The Papers library","text":"The Papers panel (⌘⌥2) holds the papers this project cites, with how many times each is cited. Open one to read it beside your draft, or drag it into the editor to cite it. Filter papers narrows the list, and Sort and group chooses its order and grouping. To add papers, click + in the Search or add papers box (or the Add papers button in the panel's toolbar), or just paste into that box. Paste anything you have: DOIs, arXiv IDs, links, titles or whole BibTeX entries, one per line. Then choose Add papers (it reads Add 3 papers once Lattice has found them). Lattice looks each one up and writes it into your .bib file. Other ways in, from the same sheet: Enter manually: type the fields yourself. Linked library…: link a .bib exported from Zotero, Paperpile or another manager, and take in its later changes after you review them. Zotero…: see below. Also save full text (Markdown): keep a readable copy of each paper in the project. Discover literature, beside Add papers, searches literature services for papers to add."},{"href":"citations.html#check-references","title":"Citations and papers › Check references","text":"Check references (in the Papers panel and the command palette) checks every reference against its published record (Semantic Scholar first, then other sources for what it can't answer). Choose Check all, or tick references and choose Check selected. The report sorts them by what each asks of you: a fix to apply, a candidate to review, a check that couldn't finish, and the ones verified. Apply writes one fix into your .bib; Apply all takes every fix that only fills in or publishes an entry, and leaves disputed values to you. The report is kept, so it is there next time."},{"href":"citations.html#zotero","title":"Citations and papers › Zotero","text":"You don't need to change anything in Zotero, and it can be open or closed. In Add papers, choose Zotero…. Open My Library or a folder; turn on Include subfolders to see everything below it. Tick the papers you want, or Select all, and choose Add N papers. Papers already in the library show In Papers; you can still tick one to bring in what it lacks, and the button then reads Add PDF, Add annotations or Add PDF and annotations. Annotations you made on a paper's PDF in Zotero come along into its note (notes/<key>.md), each with a link to its page. Lattice only reads from Zotero; it never writes back. If Lattice can't find your library, choose its folder with Choose Zotero folder…. More detail, including connecting Zotero to the Agent, is in Zotero, Heptabase and the Agent panel."},{"href":"getting-started.html#install-lattice","title":"Getting started › Install Lattice","text":"Download the .dmg from the latest release and drag Lattice into Applications. On an Intel Mac, take the file ending in _x64.dmg. With Homebrew: brew install --cask lattice. Open Lattice. It updates itself: when a new version is ready, Lattice › Restart to Update… installs it. Settings › Editor & builds › App updates shows the version you run and has Check for updates. Lattice needs macOS 14 or later. Windows and Linux builds are experimental, run the classic app and have no Agent; this guide describes the Mac app."},{"href":"getting-started.html#the-native-lattice","title":"Getting started › The native Lattice","text":"On a Mac, Lattice is the native app: the Mac draws it itself instead of as a web page, on Apple silicon and Intel Macs alike. Since version 0.20.0 it replaces the classic version, which Windows and Linux keep. Updating a classic copy installs the native one in its place with nothing to choose, and a classic copy of 0.20.0 or later installs it the moment it starts, with a short notice while it downloads. What comes with you: your recent projects, settings, themes, plugins, project templates, Agent skills and Overleaf links. Your Agent conversations come along too: each time the native version starts its Agent, it adds the classic version's conversations it doesn't have yet, and says how many in a short notice. Your project folders are untouched either way. If you had switched back: versions 0.19.0 to 0.19.4 could switch back to the classic version. If you did, the native version starts with its own settings and adds what you did in the classic version since: the projects you opened, the settings you changed there (a setting the native version already held at a value of its own keeps it, including one it brought over from the classic version the first time), and the themes, plugins, templates and skills you added. What's different: the native version checks spelling with macOS's own checker and has no grammar check or dictionaries for other languages. There is no way back to the classic version on a Mac; its data stays on your Mac, untouched."},{"href":"getting-started.html#set-up-tex","title":"Getting started › Set up TeX","text":"Lattice builds PDFs with your own TeX installation (MacTeX or TeX Live). If you have none, Lattice offers to install BasicTeX for you: it is the first step of the Get started checklist, and a build that finds no TeX offers it too. A missing LaTeX package found during a build can also be installed from the build problem itself. Typst documents need no TeX at all; see Typst documents. To check your setup at any time, open Settings › TeX doctor. It lists every tool a build uses and what the open project needs (its root document, bibliography, venue packages and fonts). Ready to compile means you are set. If something is red, Install required tools installs it. Adding papers and looking up citations need a small Python tool (uv), which the same button installs; the Get started panel's Install paper tools does the same (about 45 MB)."},{"href":"getting-started.html#open-or-create-a-project","title":"Getting started › Open or create a project","text":"The project menu (the project name at the top left of the window) and the File menu hold the ways to open and start projects: Open another folder (⌘O, File › Open…): any folder with a .tex or .typ file. Lattice works on it in place. In the project menu, Open a folder in this window opens it as a tab of the window you're in. New project (⌘⇧N): the Create a research project dialog. Give it a Project name, pick a Venue template (blank article, Chinese article with ctexart, NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, ACL, CVPR and the CVPR rebuttal, AAAI, IEEE conference and journal, ACM, Tsinghua and USTC theses, or Blank Typst document) or one of My templates, then Choose parent folder…. The folder you pick holds the project's own folder, and the dialog shows what it will make (for example Will create ProjectA/Manuscript/) before you press Create project. It warns you when the parent folder already has the project's name, so you don't end up with Manuscript/Manuscript/. Clone repository…: a Git repository from GitHub or elsewhere. Open from Overleaf: see Overleaf sync. File › Import Overleaf ZIP…: a project downloaded from Overleaf as a ZIP. Recent projects are at the top of the project menu and in File › Open Recent. A window can hold several projects as tabs in the title bar."},{"href":"getting-started.html#a-repository-with-several-manuscripts","title":"Getting started › A repository with several manuscripts","text":"A project is just a folder, so it can be a whole repository: analysis code, data and one or more papers side by side. Opening the repository's root as the project lets the Agent work across all of it, for example checking a number in the Results against the output in analysis/ and then revising the paper in the same conversation. Start each paper with New manuscript from template…. It is on a folder's right-click menu, on the Project panel's own right-click menu, in its + menu and in the command palette (⌘K). Pick a Template (the same ones New project offers, your saved templates included) and a Folder (Manuscript to start with), and optionally tick Set as main document so Build compiles it. Create manuscript writes the template's files into that folder and opens its main file. The project that is open stays open, with its settings, history and Agent conversations; no second project is made. If any of the files is already there, nothing is written and the dialog lists the files in the way."},{"href":"getting-started.html#the-get-started-checklist","title":"Getting started › The Get started checklist","text":"Guided tutorial in the project menu opens a sample paper beside Get started, a six-step checklist: install LaTeX, change the title and build, jump between PDF and source, cite a paper, ask the Agent to revise a paragraph, and start your own paper. Each step has Show me, and steps you do on your own tick themselves. To reopen the checklist later, run Show Get started panel from the command palette (⌘K)."},{"href":"history.html","title":"History and Compare versions","text":"Lattice keeps a history of every change to your project, whoever made it: you, the Agent, or a co-author on Overleaf. Open it with the Project history button in the title bar (the clock), or Open project history in the command palette (⌘K)."},{"href":"history.html#undo-a-change","title":"History and Compare versions › Undo a change","text":"History has three views, four for a project linked to Overleaf: Everything lists every change newest first: your edits, the Agent's, saved versions and Overleaf updates. Activity lists Lattice's own edits and the Agent's turns, filtered by All, You, Agent or Citations. An Agent turn can be undone as a whole: Undo this Agent turn's file changes. Versions lists the versions you saved (below). Overleaf lists the updates from Overleaf, for a linked project. Click a change to see its diff, then Restore this file to put the file back as it was before that change. A restore is itself a new entry, so it can be undone too."},{"href":"history.html#save-versions","title":"History and Compare versions › Save versions","text":"Named versions use Git. The first time, Versions offers Enable version tracking, which makes the project a Git repository (an existing repository is used as it is). Then: Save version records the project now, with a name you give it. Each version has Compare with now and Restore project to this version."},{"href":"history.html#compare-versions","title":"History and Compare versions › Compare versions","text":"Choose Compare… in the History toolbar, then pick a From and a To version (Now is the project as it is on disk). Lattice lists every changed file. Prose shows changed words, which is easier to read for text; Source shows the line diff. Export PDF with changes… writes a PDF with the differences marked, through latexdiff from your TeX installation. With BasicTeX you may need sudo tlmgr install latexdiff first."},{"href":"history.html#track-changes","title":"History and Compare versions › Track changes","text":"The Track changes button in the title bar turns on review mode: your edits from then on are marked in the source as you type (the button then reads Stop tracking changes). The review bar steps through them with Previous change and Next change, and has Accept, Reject, Accept all, Reject all and Show changes in the PDF. It works without Overleaf."},{"href":"overleaf.html","title":"Overleaf sync","text":"Lattice syncs with Overleaf both ways. Your co-authors keep working on Overleaf and don't need Lattice; their edits, comments, chat and tracked changes show up in Lattice as they happen."},{"href":"overleaf.html#connect","title":"Overleaf sync › Connect","text":"Open Settings › Overleaf and choose Connect to Overleaf. You sign in on Overleaf's own page; Lattice never sees your password. Once connected, the page shows Connected as your account, with Sign out."},{"href":"overleaf.html#open-an-overleaf-project","title":"Overleaf sync › Open an Overleaf project","text":"Choose Open from Overleaf in the project menu or the File menu, or click the cloud button in the title bar (Open a project from Overleaf). Search your projects, or paste a project's link. Projects shared with you as invitations are listed too; accept the invitation on Overleaf first. Show archived lists archived projects as well. Under Download to, Choose… where the local copy goes (Documents/Overleaf Projects to start with), then Open, or Open in this window to open it as a tab of the window you're in. Lattice downloads the project into an ordinary folder and keeps that folder linked. A project you already have a linked copy of says Opens your local copy at its folder, and opening it syncs that copy instead of downloading again. If the download folder already holds files that aren't linked to Overleaf, Lattice asks first: Link this folder links them, keeping files that differ side by side and overwriting nothing; Download a new copy downloads a fresh copy instead."},{"href":"overleaf.html#put-a-local-project-on-overleaf","title":"Overleaf sync › Put a local project on Overleaf","text":"With the project open, choose Open from Overleaf and, in the same dialog, Upload this project to Overleaf. Give it a name, choose Upload and connect, and confirm. The folder stays where it is and is linked to the new Overleaf project."},{"href":"overleaf.html#how-sync-behaves","title":"Overleaf sync › How sync behaves","text":"Set in Settings › Overleaf: Sync mode: Live sync sends and receives edits as you type. Manual syncs only when you choose Sync now (in the status bar): it first opens Review changes, which lists the files that would change with a diff of each, and Apply applies them. A badge says New changes on Overleaf when there is something to take in. When you delete a file here: Ask before deleting (the default), Delete on Overleaf too, or Keep it on Overleaf. Overleaf's history keeps a copy either way. Pause syncing stops syncing until you choose Resume syncing; the page also shows when it Last synced. The status bar says whether a file has reached Overleaf: Saved and synced, or Saved, syncing… while it is on its way. When the same file changed on both sides between syncs, Lattice merges the two against the last synced version: prose (.tex, Markdown, text) sentence by sentence, other text files line by line. Edits to different sentences, even in the same paragraph, both survive. Only a spot you both rewrote is marked, in the file itself, with your text and Overleaf's tinted side by side and Keep mine, Keep theirs and Keep both on it; the sync opens the first marked file there. The text from before the merge is kept under .research/overleaf-conflicts/, which never syncs. When a figure or another binary file changed on both sides, the newer copy stays and the other is set aside. A notice says whose copy stayed, with Use mine or Use Overleaf's to swap. The PDF that a main document builds never syncs, for every main document in the project. A PDF your source includes as a figure still syncs. Overleaf refuses some file names, such as one with a backslash or an asterisk, or one starting or ending with a space. Such a file stays on this Mac and everything else keeps syncing; a warning names the file and the rule it breaks. Rename it to sync it. Uploading a project with such a name to Overleaf asks you to rename them first."},{"href":"overleaf.html#comments-suggestions-and-chat","title":"Overleaf sync › Comments, suggestions and chat","text":"Comments and suggestions from Overleaf appear in the margin of the source and in the Overleaf panel (Comments, Suggestions and Chat). For a linked project, View › Margin Review chooses how much the margin shows: Auto, Markers Only or Off."},{"href":"plugin-development.html","title":"Your first plugin","text":"A plugin adds commands to the command palette (⌘K). It is a folder holding a manifest, lattice-plugin.toml, and a Node script. When you run one of its commands, Lattice starts the script in a sandbox, sends it the command over stdin, and shows what it answers: a message, a list to pick from, or problems in your files. A plugin never draws its own UI or writes files; it asks Lattice to, and the writer confirms. This page builds a plugin called Hello, step by step. Plugin manifest and API is the reference for everything it uses, and Publishing a plugin takes it to the plugin market. You need: a Mac with Lattice 0.18 or later. Plugins run only on macOS for now, and Lattice runs them with the Node it ships, so the people who install your plugin need nothing else; Node 22 or later in Terminal (node --version), to try the plugin outside Lattice and to pack it."},{"href":"plugin-development.html#1-make-the-folder","title":"Your first plugin › 1. Make the folder","text":"mkdir hello && cd hello curl -fsSLO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lattice-writer/lattice/main/plugin-sdk/lattice-plugin.mjs lattice-plugin.mjs is the SDK: one MIT-licensed file with no dependencies that speaks the protocol for you. It lives in your folder because a plugin can read only its own folder. TypeScript types for it are in plugin-sdk (index.d.ts and api.d.ts)."},{"href":"plugin-development.html#2-write-the-manifest","title":"Your first plugin › 2. Write the manifest","text":"Create lattice-plugin.toml: schema = 1 id = \"io.github.yourname.hello\" name = \"Hello\" version = \"0.1.0\" api = 1 license = \"MIT\" description = \"Greets you and counts the words you selected.\" runtime = \"node\" main = \"main.mjs\" [[commands]] id = \"greet\" title = \"Say hello\" description = \"Greets you by name.\" input = { kind = \"text\", placeholder = \"Your name\" } id is the plugin's identity for good: reverse-domain and lower case, under a name you own, such as io.github.<your GitHub name>.<plugin>. app.lattice.* is reserved for Lattice's own plugins. api = 1 is the lowest plugin API level the plugin needs. Lattice 0.18 speaks level 1. Each [[commands]] entry becomes \"Hello: \" in the palette. input = { kind = \"text\" } asks the writer to type something before the command runs."},{"href":"plugin-development.html#3-write-the-command","title":"Your first plugin › 3. Write the command","text":"Create main.mjs: import { definePlugin, PluginError } from \"./lattice-plugin.mjs\"; definePlugin({ commands: { async greet({ input, context }) { const name = input?.trim(); if (!name) throw new PluginError(\"Type your name first.\"); return { kind: \"message\", summary: `Hello, ${name}`, markdown: `Hello, **${name}**! This is *${context.project.name}*.`, }; }, }, }); Each key under commands is a command id from the manifest. A handler gets { input, context, host, signal } and returns a result. Throwing PluginError shows its message to the writer instead."},{"href":"plugin-development.html#4-try-it-in-terminal","title":"Your first plugin › 4. Try it in Terminal","text":"Lattice talks to a plugin in JSON-RPC, one JSON message per line. You can play Lattice's part yourself: ( echo '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"hostApiLevel\":1,\"hostApiCompat\":1}}' echo '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"command/run\",\"params\":{\"command\":\"greet\",\"input\":\"Ada\",\"context\":{\"project\":{\"name\":\"thesis\"}}}}' sleep 1 ) | node main.mjs The plugin answers both messages: {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"result\":{\"apiLevel\":1}} {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"result\":{\"kind\":\"message\",\"summary\":\"Hello, Ada\",\"markdown\":\"Hello, **Ada**! This is *thesis*.\"}} With \"input\":\"\" it answers {\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Type your name first.\"} instead. The sleep 1 keeps stdin open while the plugin answers: the SDK exits when stdin closes, because that is how Lattice ends a run."},{"href":"plugin-development.html#5-install-it-from-its-folder","title":"Your first plugin › 5. Install it from its folder","text":"Open Settings › Plugins. Next to Install from folder…, choose Choose folder… and pick the hello folder. Hello appears under Installed, marked Developer and turned off. Turn it on: Lattice asks \"Turn on Hello?\" and lists what it will be able to do, here \"No access beyond the project’s name\". Choose Turn on. Open a project, press ⌘K, type \"hello\" and choose Hello: Say hello. Type your name and press Return. The palette shows \"Hello, Ada! This is Understanding Attention.\" Lattice runs the folder in place and reads it again on every run, so after you save main.mjs or the manifest, just run the command again."},{"href":"plugin-development.html#6-read-the-project","title":"Your first plugin › 6. Read the project","text":"Without permissions, a plugin learns only the project's name. To read more, it asks in the manifest, and the writer agrees when turning it on. Replace lattice-plugin.toml with this version, which adds a [permissions] table and a second command: schema = 1 id = \"io.github.yourname.hello\" name = \"Hello\" version = \"0.1.0\" api = 1 license = \"MIT\" description = \"Greets you and counts the words you selected.\" runtime = \"node\" main = \"main.mjs\" [permissions] project-read = [\"context\"] [[commands]] id = \"greet\" title = \"Say hello\" description = \"Greets you by name.\" input = { kind = \"text\", placeholder = \"Your name\" } [[commands]] id = \"count\" title = \"Count words in the selection\" project-read = [\"context\"] gives the plugin the main document, the active file, the bibliography paths, the selection (up to 12,000 characters) and the cursor. Then replace main.mjs to use them: import { definePlugin, PluginError } from \"./lattice-plugin.mjs\"; definePlugin({ commands: { async greet({ input, context, host }) { const name = input?.trim(); if (!name) throw new PluginError(\"Type your name first.\"); host.log(\"info\", `greeting ${name}`); const file = context.project.activeFile ?? \"no file\"; return { kind: \"message\", summary: `Hello, ${name}`, markdown: `Hello, **${name}**! You are editing \\`${file}\\` in *${context.project.name}*.`, }; }, async count({ context }) { const selection = context.selection; if (!selection?.text) throw new PluginError(\"Select some text first.\"); const words = selection.text.split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean).length; return { kind: \"items\", summary: `${words} words selected`, items: [ { id: \"words\", title: `${words} words`, subtitle: `${selection.file}, line ${selection.from.line}`, actions: [ { type: \"copy\", title: \"Copy the count\", text: String(words) }, { type: \"reveal\", title: \"Show the selection\", file: selection.file, line: selection.from.line }, ], }, ], }; }, }, }); count returns a list instead of a message. Each item carries actions, verbs Lattice performs for the plugin: the first runs on Return, here copying the count. Try count with a selection: ( echo '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"hostApiLevel\":1,\"hostApiCompat\":1}}' echo '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"command/run\",\"params\":{\"command\":\"count\",\"context\":{\"project\":{\"name\":\"thesis\"},\"selection\":{\"file\":\"main.tex\",\"text\":\"Attention is all you need\",\"from\":{\"line\":12,\"column\":1},\"to\":{\"line\":12,\"column\":26}}}}}' sleep 1 ) | node main.mjs Back in Lattice, run Hello: Say hello again. It doesn't run: the plugin now asks for more than you allowed, so the palette says \"This plugin asks for new permissions. Review them in Settings › Plugins.\" Choose Open Settings. Hello's card says Asks for new permissions; choose Review…, check the list, and choose Turn on. Every installed plugin works this way, so a plugin can never gain access without the writer seeing it. Now Hello: Say hello names the file you're editing, and Hello: Count words in the selection counts what you selected; Return copies the count."},{"href":"plugin-development.html#7-read-its-logs","title":"Your first plugin › 7. Read its logs","text":"host.log(level, message) writes to Lattice's log, ~/Library/Logs/app.leo1oel.researchwriter/lattice.log, as a line tagged lattice::plugins with your plugin's id. Follow it while you work: tail -f ~/Library/Logs/app.leo1oel.researchwriter/lattice.log | grep --line-buffered lattice::plugins Running Hello: Say hello adds a line ending in io.github.yourname.hello: greeting Ada. Settings › Logs › Open log folder opens the folder. console.log and stderr. The SDK sends console.log to stderr, because stdout carries the protocol. When the plugin stops without answering, Lattice shows the end of its stderr in the palette. Otherwise stderr goes to the log only at the debug level, so start Lattice from Terminal to see it: LATTICE_LOG=debug /Applications/Lattice.app/Contents/MacOS/research-writer Never print to stdout yourself. A line on stdout that isn't JSON-RPC ends the run, and the palette says “The plugin sent something Lattice couldn’t read”."},{"href":"plugin-development.html#what39s-next","title":"Your first plugin › What's next","text":"Plugin manifest and API: every permission, host call, result and action. Publishing a plugin: pack Hello, list it in the plugin market, and ship updates. Two real plugins to copy: bib-check (diagnostics for .bib files) and zotero-search (search Zotero and cite)."},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html","title":"Publishing a plugin","text":"The plugin market in Settings › Plugins › Browse lists plugins from the Lattice registry, a GitHub repository where each plugin is one small entry added by pull request. The entry pins a tag of your source repository and the SHA-256 of your plugin folder packed at that tag. The registry never takes a zip you built: its CI packs your tagged folder itself and publishes that zip only when the hashes match, so what a reviewer read is byte for byte what people install. This page takes the Hello plugin from Your first plugin to the market. Themes go through the same registry; see Making a theme."},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#registry-addresses","title":"Publishing a plugin › Registry addresses","text":"This table and the commands under Signing are the only places in this guide that name the registry, so they are what changes when the registry moves to the lattice-writer GitHub organization. What Where The registry repository lattice-writer/lattice-plugins Its README: entry format and CI checks README.md The review checklist REVIEW.md The pull request checklist pull_request_template.md The kill switch blocked.json The signed index Lattice reads index.json, with index.json.minisig The public signing key keys/primary.pub"},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#before-you-publish","title":"Publishing a plugin › Before you publish","text":"The registry checks these, and a reviewer reads the rest: The plugin is a node plugin whose scripts are readable source: no minified or generated code (no line over 1,000 characters), no eval, new Function or vm, and no fetching and running remote code. Vendor any dependency as readable source inside the folder. The manifest has a description and a license Lattice accepts (see the manifest). The folder has a README.md and a LICENSE file. The README names every network origin and network-local port with the reason, says what each secret is for, and says plainly if the plugin sends project content off the Mac. The id is yours and doesn't impersonate anyone: no \"Lattice\" or \"Overleaf\" names without permission, no look-alike ids. You ran every command in Lattice from a folder install. For Hello, add a README.md: # Hello A Lattice plugin that greets you and counts the words you selected. ## What it reads - `project-read = [\"context\"]`: the project's name, the active file and the selected text, to put them in its answers. It makes no network connections and sends nothing off your Mac. and a LICENSE with the MIT licence text and your name."},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#1-tag-your-source","title":"Publishing a plugin › 1. Tag your source","text":"Push the plugin folder to a public GitHub repository, here https://github.com/yourname/lattice-hello with the plugin at its root, then tag the commit you want listed: git tag v0.1.0 git push origin v0.1.0 The plugin can also live in a subfolder of a bigger repository; the entry's path then names that folder."},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#2-pack-it-and-get-its-hash","title":"Publishing a plugin › 2. Pack it and get its hash","text":"Pack a clean checkout of the tag with Lattice's pack script: curl -fsSLO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lattice-writer/lattice/main/plugin-sdk/scripts/pack-plugin.mjs git clone --quiet --depth 1 --branch v0.1.0 https://github.com/yourname/lattice-hello release node pack-plugin.mjs release dist/hello-0.1.0.zip It prints the zip's path and its SHA-256: /Users/you/dist/hello-0.1.0.zip sha256 c6d59e1c411f807169880145090894fb684ecdf624f13351a97f60e48399db87 The pack is reproducible: the same files give the same bytes on any Mac or CI runner, whatever their timestamps, owners or the order the disk lists them in. It takes the regular files under the folder, sorted by path, leaves out tests (*.test.mjs) and anything whose name starts with a dot (so .git stays out), and stores each one uncompressed with a fixed 1980-01-01 time and permissions 0644, or 0755 for an executable. Entries are stored rather than compressed because compressed bytes differ between zlib versions, so a compressed zip would hash differently on your Mac and in the registry's CI. unzip -v dist/hello-0.1.0.zip lists every entry as Stored. Pack the clean clone, not your working folder. CI packs exactly what git has at the tag, so an untracked file in your folder, or a file whose executable bit git doesn't record, gives a hash CI won't reproduce. Packing twice gives the same hash; if it doesn't, something in the folder is changing."},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#3-add-the-entry","title":"Publishing a plugin › 3. Add the entry","text":"Fork the registry (addresses), and in your fork add plugins/<id>/entry.json, here plugins/io.github.yourname.hello/entry.json: { \"id\": \"io.github.yourname.hello\", \"version\": \"0.1.0\", \"repository\": \"https://github.com/yourname/lattice-hello\", \"tag\": \"v0.1.0\", \"commit\": \"<the full 40-character sha the tag points at>\", \"path\": \".\", \"sha256\": \"c6d59e1c411f807169880145090894fb684ecdf624f13351a97f60e48399db87\", \"author\": \"Your Name\", \"categories\": [\"writing\"] } id and version equal the manifest's, and the folder name equals id. commit is what git rev-parse \"v0.1.0^{commit}\" prints in your repository. path is the plugin folder inside the repository, or \".\" for its root. sha256 is what pack-plugin.mjs printed. categories lists up to four lower-case words, such as citations, bibliography or writing. Run the same checks as CI before you open the pull request. In your fork's checkout, with its main up to date and Node 22 or later: npm ci node scripts/validate.mjs --base origin/main For a new plugin it prints: ok plugins/io.github.yourname.hello/entry.json note: asks for: {\"projectRead\":[\"context\"],\"networkLocal\":[],\"network\":[],\"secrets\":[]} note: new plugin: needs a human review per REVIEW.md (first submission) A wrong hash fails with sha256 mismatch: the entry says …, the pack of . at <commit> is …. Then open the pull request, one entry per pull request, and fill in its checklist."},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#4-what-ci-checks","title":"Publishing a plugin › 4. What CI checks","text":"On every pull request, the registry's validate.mjs fetches only your tag, requires it to point at commit, and reads the folder from git's object store at that commit. Then it: refuses symlinks and submodules, and keeps within Lattice's limits: 10,000 files, 200 MiB unpacked, a 50 MiB zip; packs the folder and compares the SHA-256 with the entry's; checks the manifest as Lattice does (schema, id, version, API level, name, permissions and commands), plus the market's rules: runtime node, a description, an accepted licence, a README and a licence file; rejects scripts with lines over 1,000 characters or that use eval, new Function or vm; for an update, checks that version went up, prints what the new version asks for beyond the published one, and flags a changed repository or runtime; checks that blocked.json is well formed."},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#5-review","title":"Publishing a plugin › 5. Review","text":"CI does the mechanical half; a maintainer (today, Leo) does the rest with REVIEW.md and writes the answers in the pull request. A human reviews every first submission, and any update that changes the repository or owner, grows the permissions, or changes the runtime. Other updates merge after green CI and a 24-hour cooling period, during which any maintainer can hold them. The reviewer reads your source at the pinned commit and asks: Is the id or name impersonating someone? Is the description accurate, the licence present, and the repository real and public? A port credits its original author. Do the permissions fit the stated purpose, with every origin, port and secret explained in the README? Does it send project content off the Mac, and if so, do the README and description say so plainly? Is the source readable, with no fetching and running remote code, and no dependencies that aren't vendored as readable source? Does it duplicate a built-in feature in a misleading way?"},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#6-signing","title":"Publishing a plugin › 6. Signing","text":"You don't sign anything. Once your pull request merges, the registry's publish workflow packs every entry, builds index.json and blocked.json, signs both with the registry's minisign key, and publishes them with the zips to GitHub Pages. The signing job runs only from main and waits for Leo's approval on every run, and the private key exists only as a secret of that job's protected environment. Lattice checks both signatures against public keys built into the app, refuses an index older than the last one it verified, and checks every zip's SHA-256 and manifest against the index before installing it. So neither a compromised download server nor someone with access to your GitHub account can change what people install. To check the published index yourself: curl -fsSLO https://lattice-writer.github.io/lattice-plugins/index.json curl -fsSLO https://lattice-writer.github.io/lattice-plugins/index.json.minisig curl -fsSLO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lattice-writer/lattice-plugins/main/keys/primary.pub minisign -V -p primary.pub -m index.json It prints Signature and comment signature verified and a trusted comment like lattice-plugins file:index.json serial:2. (brew install minisign installs minisign.) Your plugin shows in Browse after the next publish, marked Reviewed once installed."},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#updates-and-permission-changes","title":"Publishing a plugin › Updates and permission changes","text":"To ship a new version: Raise version in lattice-plugin.toml, commit, and tag the commit (v0.2.0). Pack the clean tag again, as in step 2. In your entry, change version, tag, commit and sha256, and open a pull request. Versions only go up, and the repository should stay the same: a move needs a human review. When an update asks for more, CI says exactly what is new, for example: ok plugins/io.github.yourname.hello/entry.json note: asks for: {\"projectRead\":[\"context\"],\"networkLocal\":[],\"network\":[\"https://api.crossref.org\"],\"secrets\":[]} note: new network: https://api.crossref.org note: permissions grew: needs a human review What people see in Settings › Plugins: An update that asks for nothing new is applied by Update all, and the plugin stays on. An update that asks for more waits on its own card, which lists only what is new, with Allow and update or keep the version they have. So keep permissions stable, and when you need a new one, explain it in the README and in the pull request. A folder install behaves the same way while you develop: a new permission stops the plugin until you choose Review… and turn it on again."},{"href":"plugin-publishing.html#the-kill-switch","title":"Publishing a plugin › The kill switch","text":"If a published version turns out to be harmful or broken, the registry can turn it off on every Mac. blocked.json lists blocked versions and is signed and published with the index: [ { \"id\": \"io.github.yourname.hello\", \"versions\": \"<=0.1.0\", \"action\": \"disable\", \"reason\": \"Crashes on projects without a main document; update to 0.1.1.\", \"url\": \"https://github.com/yourname/lattice-hello/issues/1\" } ] versions is a semver range, or \"*\" for every version. \"disable\" turns installed copies off, refuses new installs of those versions, and shows the reason on the plugin's card with Learn more linking to url. \"warn\" keeps the plugin working and shows the reason as a warning. Lattice never deletes the plugin's files or data folder, and it never touches folder installs. Removing an entry from plugins/ only stops new installs. Installed copies keep working unless they are also blocked. To pull one of your own versions, open a pull request that adds an item to blocked.json and publish a fixed version beside it; anyone who finds a harmful plugin can open an issue or pull request the same way. It takes effect on the next signed publish."},{"href":"plugin-reference.html","title":"Plugin manifest and API","text":"This page lists what a plugin can declare, ask for, call and return, with the rules the plugin market adds. The complete protocol, with every field and error code, is docs/plugins.md; the schema it is generated from is plugin-api/schema/level-1.json."},{"href":"plugin-reference.html#the-manifest","title":"Plugin manifest and API › The manifest","text":"lattice-plugin.toml sits at the root of the plugin folder. Key Required What it is schema yes The manifest format: 1. id yes Reverse-domain, lower case, unique, never changed. app.lattice.* is reserved. name yes Shown in Settings and before each command in the palette. At most 80 characters. version yes The plugin's own version, semver (1.2.3). api yes The lowest plugin API level it needs: 1. license yes An SPDX id. The market and URL installs accept 0BSD, Apache-2.0, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, BSL-1.0, GPL-3.0(-only/-or-later), ISC, LGPL-3.0(-only/-or-later), MIT, MPL-2.0, Unlicense and Zlib. description for the market One or two sentences, at most 500 characters. repository no An https link to the source. runtime yes \"node\", run with the Node Lattice ships (Node 24). \"exec\" runs a native executable per target triple ([targets]), but the market lists node plugins only. main for node The entry script, relative to the folder. [permissions] no What the plugin may reach; see below. [[commands]] yes One to 32 commands. Each command has an id (lower-case letters, digits and dashes), a title (at most 80 characters), an optional description, and an optional input: { kind = \"none\" } (the default) runs at once, { kind = \"text\", placeholder = \"…\" } asks the writer to type first. Unknown top-level keys are ignored, so a manifest written for a newer Lattice still loads. An unknown key under [permissions] is an error: it asks for something this Lattice can't enforce."},{"href":"plugin-reference.html#permissions","title":"Plugin manifest and API › Permissions","text":"A plugin reaches only what its manifest declares and the writer allowed when turning it on. Lattice enforces this with a macOS sandbox and by checking every host call; it isn't a promise the plugin makes. Permission What it allows What the writer is asked to allow project-read = [\"context\"] Main document, active file, bibliography paths, selection (at most 12,000 characters) and cursor, in every run's context Read the open file, selection and cursor project-read = [\"bibliography\"] Parsed .bib entries through host.bibliographyEntries() Read the project’s bibliography network-local = [23119] Direct connections to those ports on this Mac only (at most 8) Connect to localhost:23119 network = [\"https://api.crossref.org\"] Requests to those https origins through host.fetch() (at most 16, no paths) Send requests to https://api.crossref.org secrets = [\"api-key\"] Values the writer saves in the plugin's card, kept in the Keychain, read with host.secret() (at most 8 names) Use the saved secret “api-key” A plugin with none of these shows “No access beyond the project’s name”. It can still read its own folder, read and write its data folder (LATTICE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR), and learn the project's name. It never gets the writer's environment, API keys or other files, and it can't read project files beyond the above. Ask for the least that does the job. A plugin that asks for more in an update stops until the writer allows it again (see Publishing a plugin), and the market review asks why each permission is needed."},{"href":"plugin-reference.html#a-run","title":"Plugin manifest and API › A run","text":"Each command runs in a new process: Lattice sends initialize; the SDK answers with the API level. Lattice sends command/run with the command id, the input and the context. The handler may call the host and send logs and progress. The handler returns; Lattice closes stdin and stops the process. So nothing survives between runs except what the plugin writes to its data folder. A run that passes 120 seconds is stopped. When the writer cancels, or time runs out, the handler's signal aborts: pass it to anything long-running. The process gets PATH=/usr/bin:/bin, HOME and TMPDIR inside the data folder, LANG, LATTICE_PLUGIN_ID, LATTICE_PLUGIN_API_LEVEL and LATTICE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR."},{"href":"plugin-reference.html#calling-lattice","title":"Plugin manifest and API › Calling Lattice","text":"A handler's host has these methods: Method Needs Returns host.bibliographyEntries(file?) project-read = [\"bibliography\"] { files: [{ path, entries: [{ key, type, fields, line }], error? }] }, field names in lower case host.fetch(url or { url, method?, headers?, body? }) the URL's origin in network { status, headers, body }; bodies up to 4 MiB, 30 seconds host.secret(name) the name in secrets the saved value, or null host.log(level, message) nothing writes to Lattice's log; level is error, warn, info or debug host.progress(message, fraction?) nothing shows message in the palette while the command runs A call without its permission rejects with HostError, code -32001. For example, with network = [\"https://api.crossref.org\"]: const response = await host.fetch(`https://api.crossref.org/works/${encodeURIComponent(doi)}`); if (response.status !== 200) throw new PluginError(`Crossref answered ${response.status}.`); const work = JSON.parse(response.body).message;"},{"href":"plugin-reference.html#results","title":"Plugin manifest and API › Results","text":"Return one of three kinds. Each may carry a summary, plain text that stands for the whole result in logs and notifications. A message: { kind: \"message\", markdown }. CommonMark without raw HTML. A list: { kind: \"items\", items: [{ id, title, subtitle?, detail?, badges?, actions? }] }. The writer picks an item; its first action runs on Return. Problems: { kind: \"diagnostics\", items: [{ id, file, line?, severity, code?, message, fixes? }] }. severity is error, warning or info; code is a stable rule name such as duplicate-key; fixes are actions. To show an error instead, throw new PluginError(\"What went wrong and what to do.\")."},{"href":"plugin-reference.html#actions","title":"Plugin manifest and API › Actions","text":"A plugin never edits anything itself. It attaches actions, and Lattice performs them, asking the writer first for every change to a file: type Arguments What Lattice does insertCitation key Inserts \\cite{key} at the cursor addBibEntry bibtex, file? Shows the entry and adds it to the bibliography after the writer confirms replaceBibEntry key, bibtex, file? Shows the change and replaces the entry after the writer confirms reveal file, line? Opens the project file at the line openUrl url (http or https) Opens it in the browser copy text Copies it Each action also takes an optional title. Lattice checks every action before showing it: paths must stay inside the project, URLs must be http or https, and BibTeX must be exactly one valid entry. Anything else is dropped."},{"href":"plugin-reference.html#compatibility","title":"Plugin manifest and API › Compatibility","text":"Plugin API levels only add things: new methods, optional fields, result kinds and action types. A plugin that declares api = 1 keeps working on every later Lattice until level 1 is retired, which is announced at least two minor releases ahead in docs/plugins.md. Read only the fields you know, since results and contexts may gain fields."},{"href":"plugins-and-themes.html#themes","title":"Plugins and themes › Themes","text":"Open Settings › Appearance. Under Theme: Color theme picks light, dark or the system's appearance. Palette picks Lattice's own colors or a theme you installed. Tint, Accent and Translucency adjust the colors and the frosted window background. Theme files has Browse themes… (themes others have published: choose Install on one, then pick it under Palette), Install from file… (a theme file you were given), Show themes folder (where your installed theme files live) and Remove theme."},{"href":"plugins-and-themes.html#code-themes","title":"Plugins and themes › Code themes","text":"The editor's syntax colors are set separately under Code theme on the same page. The default is VS Code (its Light+ and Dark+ colors); GitHub, Weft & Warp (Lattice's own), One, Solarized, Catppuccin, Tokyo Night, Dracula, Nord and Monokai are the others. A code theme you chose stays, and a copy of Lattice that already ran the native version keeps GitHub, the default before 0.20.0, until you choose another. Light or dark decides which variant shows: Match app follows the app's appearance, or fix Light or Dark; a theme that comes in one variant only says so. Code blocks in the visual editor use the same code theme as source files. An installed theme that sets its own syntax colors wins over the code theme."},{"href":"plugins-and-themes.html#plugins","title":"Plugins and themes › Plugins","text":"Plugins add commands to the command palette (⌘K). Each runs in a sandbox and can reach only what you allow when you turn it on. Open Settings › Plugins. The page has two views, Installed and Browse, and one search field for whichever view is showing."},{"href":"plugins-and-themes.html#browse-the-plugin-market","title":"Plugins and themes › Browse the plugin market","text":"Browse lists the plugins in the Lattice plugin market. The list is signed, so Lattice refuses one that has been tampered with, and when you're offline it shows the last list it verified. Sort by most downloaded, recently updated or name, and choose Refresh to fetch the list again. Choose a plugin, or its Install… button, to see its details: who made it, its version and licence, what This plugin can do and what it can't, and the commands it adds. Then choose: Install and turn on: installs the plugin and turns it on. Install only: installs it turned off, to turn on later."},{"href":"plugins-and-themes.html#installed-plugins","title":"Plugins and themes › Installed plugins","text":"Each installed plugin shows where it came from: Reviewed for a plugin from the market, Not reviewed for one installed from a URL, Developer for a folder. Turn a plugin on or off with its switch; turning it on asks first (Turn on Name?) and lists what it will be able to do. A plugin that needs a key or a token gets a field for it on its card, with Save and Clear. Remove a plugin from its card. When an update is available, the card says so with Update…. Update all applies every update that asks for nothing new. An update that asks for more than you allowed says Asks for new permissions and waits on its own card; Review… lists only what is new, with Allow and update, or Keep the version you have (for example Keep 1.2.0). If a plugin is withdrawn from the market, Lattice turns it off and says why on its card (Learn more). Plugins run on macOS only for now."},{"href":"plugins-and-themes.html#install-a-plugin-yourself","title":"Plugins and themes › Install a plugin yourself","text":"Install from URL…: a plugin's .zip release over https, checked against the SHA-256 its author publishes. Install from folder…: for developing a plugin; Lattice runs the folder in place, so your edits apply on the next run. Writing a plugin or a theme? Start with Your first plugin or Making a theme."},{"href":"settings-and-shortcuts.html#settings","title":"Settings and shortcuts › Settings","text":"Open Lattice › Settings… (⌘,). The search box at the top finds any setting by name, and by the words you might use for it: \"frosted glass\" finds Translucency, \".aux\" finds Hide auxiliary files. Section Page What's there General Appearance Interface language (Match system, English or Simplified Chinese), interface size, sounds, notification position, tab style; font, size and line height for the source and Visual Markdown editors; Theme (color theme, palette, theme files, tint, accent, translucency); Code theme; Title bar tools. See Plugins and themes. Editor & builds Editor: keymap (default, Vim or Emacs), your name, Check spelling (macOS's checker). Builds: automatic build, auxiliary files and Hide auxiliary files, Typst support, and with a project open the compile engine, shell escape, Stop on first error and Live preview. App updates: the version and Check for updates. See Building. Agent Providers, Display, MCP, Skills, Usage & limits, Archived threads, Model endpoint, Environment. See The Agent. Integrations Overleaf, Literature services (keys for literature search), Plugins. Diagnostics TeX doctor, Logs. About Acknowledgements. ⌘+, ⌘− and ⌘0 (or View › Bigger / Smaller / Actual Size) change the interface size, the same setting as Appearance › Interface size, for every window."},{"href":"settings-and-shortcuts.html#shortcuts","title":"Settings and shortcuts › Shortcuts","text":"Press ⌘? (⌘⇧/) for Keyboard shortcuts, the full list, with a filter by name or key. Shortcuts follow the commands you have, so the list is always current. The ones worth learning first: Keys Action ⌘K Command palette: every command by name ⌘S Save and build ⌘P Quick open a file ⌘⇧K / ⌘⇧L Insert citation / reference ⌘⇧J Jump to PDF F8 / ⇧F8 Next / previous build problem ⌘⇧F Find in project ⌘⌥1, ⌘⌥2, ⌘⌥3 Project, Papers, Agent panel ⌘1 … ⌘9 Switch workspace ⌘⇧D Focus mode ⌘W / ⌘⇧T Close tab / reopen it To use Vim or Emacs keys in the editor, set Settings › Editor & builds › Editor keymap."},{"href":"theme-development.html","title":"Making a theme","text":"A theme recolors Lattice. It is data, not code: one .lattice-theme.json file that gives colors to Lattice's design roles, such as the window, the editor, text and the accent. Lattice works out every other color (hover, pressed, selection washes) from those, so a theme stays consistent in every state. A theme can't change sizes, fonts or behavior and can't run anything, so installing one asks for no permission and works on every platform."},{"href":"theme-development.html#1-write-the-file","title":"Making a theme › 1. Write the file","text":"Create meadow.lattice-theme.json: { \"$schema\": \"https://lattice-writer.github.io/lattice-plugins/schema/theme-1.json\", \"schema\": 1, \"id\": \"io.github.yourname.meadow\", \"name\": \"Meadow\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"author\": \"Your Name\", \"license\": \"MIT\", \"description\": \"Soft greens for long writing sessions.\", \"variants\": [ { \"id\": \"day\", \"name\": \"Meadow Day\", \"appearance\": \"light\", \"colors\": { \"window\": \"#eef2e6\", \"chrome\": \"#e4eadb\", \"panel\": \"#f6f8f1\", \"editor\": \"#fbfcf8\", \"text\": \"#27331f\", \"text-secondary\": \"#56624c\", \"line\": \"#27331f1f\", \"accent\": \"#4f7a28\" }, \"syntax\": { \"keyword\": \"#4f7a28\", \"comment\": \"#6f7a66\" } }, { \"id\": \"night\", \"name\": \"Meadow Night\", \"appearance\": \"dark\", \"colors\": { \"window\": \"#161b13\", \"chrome\": \"#1c2318\", \"panel\": \"#20281b\", \"editor\": \"#1a2016\", \"text\": \"#e3ead8\", \"text-secondary\": \"#a9b49c\", \"line\": \"#e3ead81f\", \"accent\": \"#9cc56b\" } } ] } The $schema line lets editors such as VS Code check the file and complete role names as you type. id is reverse-domain, lower case and yours, like a plugin's. app.lattice.* belongs to the themes that ship with Lattice. Variants: one to eight, each light or dark. Lattice uses the first variant that matches the window's appearance; with only a dark variant, the light appearance stays Lattice's own. Colors are literal #RRGGBB or #RRGGBBAA. No names, var() or other functions. Lines are usually translucent, as above. Every role is optional. A role you leave out keeps Lattice's color for that appearance, so a theme can be as small as two surfaces and an accent. An unknown role is an error. The file may be at most 256 KiB."},{"href":"theme-development.html#the-roles","title":"Making a theme › The roles","text":"Section Roles colors, surfaces window, chrome, panel, raised, input, editor, pdf-ground colors, text text, text-secondary colors, lines line, line-strong colors, accent accent, focus, selection colors, status danger, success, warning, info, annotation syntax the editor's 16 token colors, such as keyword, command and comment terminal black … white and bright-black … bright-white Lattice keeps text readable: text, status and syntax colors that don't reach a 4.5:1 contrast on their surfaces are moved, keeping their hue, until they do, and the accent is held to 4.6:1. A theme that only reads after heavy correction will look off, so check your colors yourself. The full list of roles is in docs/themes.md and the JSON Schema."},{"href":"theme-development.html#2-try-it-in-lattice","title":"Making a theme › 2. Try it in Lattice","text":"Open Settings › Appearance. Under Theme, next to Theme files, choose Install from file… and pick meadow.lattice-theme.json. Lattice installs it and chooses it: Meadow appears under Palette, checked, with its description. Switch Color theme between Light and Dark to see both variants. Lattice checks the file when you install it and says what is wrong, such as an unknown role. It keeps a copy, so after you edit the file, install it again to see the change."},{"href":"theme-development.html#3-publish-it","title":"Making a theme › 3. Publish it","text":"Themes go through the same registry as plugins, with a smaller entry. The registry's addresses are listed under Registry addresses. Put the theme file and a licence in a public GitHub repository, here https://github.com/yourname/lattice-theme-meadow, and tag the commit: git tag v1.0.0 git push origin v1.0.0 Hash the file as committed. A theme isn't packed; the hash is the file's own: shasum -a 256 meadow.lattice-theme.json In a fork of the registry, add themes/<id>/entry.json, here themes/io.github.yourname.meadow/entry.json: { \"id\": \"io.github.yourname.meadow\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"repository\": \"https://github.com/yourname/lattice-theme-meadow\", \"tag\": \"v1.0.0\", \"commit\": \"<the full 40-character sha the tag points at>\", \"path\": \"meadow.lattice-theme.json\", \"sha256\": \"<what shasum printed>\" } id and version equal the theme file's own, the folder name equals id, and path is the file inside the repository. Run npm ci and node scripts/validate.mjs --base origin/main in the fork, as for a plugin. A new theme prints ok themes/io.github.yourname.meadow/entry.json and note: new theme: needs a human review (first submission). Open the pull request and fill in the theme half of its checklist. CI fetches only your tag, requires it to point at commit, reads the file at path from that commit (a regular file of at most 256 KiB), checks its SHA-256, validates it against the schema, and checks that its id and version match the entry and its variant ids are unique. A reviewer then asks: Is the id or name impersonating someone? Is the description accurate, the licence present, and the repository real and public? A ported palette credits its original author and keeps its licence. Is it readable? A theme that only works after Lattice's contrast correction is rejected. Does it stay data? No fonts, no images, nothing outside the schema. Once merged, the theme is signed and published with the next index, like a plugin (see Signing), and it appears in Settings › Appearance › Browse themes…. Its tile there is painted from the index, so nothing downloads before someone chooses Install."},{"href":"theme-development.html#updates-and-the-kill-switch","title":"Making a theme › Updates and the kill switch","text":"To update a theme, raise version in the file, tag the new commit, and change version, tag, commit and sha256 in your entry. Versions only go up. People who installed it from Browse themes are offered the update, which keeps their choice of theme. The registry's kill switch covers themes too. When a version someone has chosen is blocked with \"disable\", Lattice draws its own colors instead, keeps the choice stored, and says \" was turned off: \" in Settings › Appearance. A \"warn\" entry leaves the theme applied and shows its warning there. Themes installed from a file are never blocked."},{"href":"troubleshooting.html#something-went-wrong","title":"Troubleshooting and FAQ › Something went wrong","text":"The build fails and I don't see why. Read the first problem in the build card; later ones often follow from it. Open Log for LaTeX's own words. Then try Clean rebuild from the command palette (⌘K), or ⇧-click the build button. See Building. \"Missing LaTeX dependency\" or a package not found. BasicTeX doesn't include every package Overleaf has. Use the problem's Install button, or Settings › TeX doctor › Install required tools. A build can't find LaTeX at all. Open Settings › TeX doctor. If it lists missing tools, install TeX from the Get started checklist or from MacTeX, then reopen the project. A document builds only with Hide auxiliary files off. Some packages (minted 2.x, for example) ignore TeX's output directory. Lattice builds such a document beside itself on its own; if it still fails, turn off Settings › Editor & builds › Hide auxiliary files. See Auxiliary files. A Typst document doesn't build. Open Settings › Editor & builds › Typst support: if it isn't installed, choose Download; if a download failed or an update left it behind, Download again. See Typst documents. Adding papers or looking up citations doesn't work. These need the small uv tool. Settings › TeX doctor shows whether uv and uvx are there; Install required tools installs them. The Agent says it isn't signed in. The panel shows which provider is missing a sign-in, for example \"Claude is not authenticated\". Sign in once in Terminal (claude auth login for Claude Code), then try again, or pick another provider under Settings › Agent › Providers. Overleaf stopped syncing. Open Settings › Overleaf. If your session expired, choose Reconnect to Overleaf; if syncing is paused, Resume syncing. A file with conflict markers pauses sync for that file until you choose Keep mine, Keep theirs or Keep both on each marked spot. A panel is gone. Panels › Reset layout brings every panel back. To undo a deleted workspace, use Undo in the notice right away. I lost text. Open History: every save, every Agent edit and every Overleaf change is there, and Restore this file puts a file back. Something else is wrong. Help › Report a Problem… saves a redacted log you can attach to an issue, and opens GitHub Issues. Settings › Logs shows the log in the app, with Export…."},{"href":"troubleshooting.html#faq","title":"Troubleshooting and FAQ › FAQ","text":"Where is my paper stored? In the folder you opened or created, as ordinary files. Lattice keeps what it adds (build files when you hide them, Overleaf conflict copies and the like) in the project's hidden .research folder and in its own app data, never in your .tex files. Do I need an AI subscription? Only for the Agent and the proofreading tools. Everything else, including builds, citations, history and Overleaf sync, works without one. See The Agent. Do my co-authors need Lattice? No. They keep working on Overleaf; see Overleaf sync. Can I go back to the classic app on my Mac? No. Since 0.20.0 every Mac runs the native app; the classic app's data stays on your Mac, untouched. See The native Lattice. I use Windows or Linux. Those builds are experimental and still run the classic app, which has no Agent. Most of this guide applies, but labels and menus differ in places. Does Lattice work offline? Yes, except for what needs the network: Overleaf sync, the Agent with a hosted model, looking up papers, the plugin and theme markets, and first-time downloads such as Typst support and Typst packages."},{"href":"typst.html","title":"Typst documents","text":"Lattice also writes and builds Typst documents (.typ files). A .typ file opens with syntax highlighting straight away. The Typst compiler is a separate one-time download, so the app stays small for writers who never use Typst."},{"href":"typst.html#download-typst-support","title":"Typst documents › Download Typst support","text":"The first time you build a Typst document, or it starts compiling as you type, Lattice asks to download Typst support, a one-time download of about 22 MB. Download fetches it, checks its signature and runs the build. Not now leaves the file as it was, with highlighting only; typing doesn't ask again until you restart Lattice, and the next build asks again. After an app update, Lattice downloads the matching version on its own. Settings › Editor & builds › Typst support shows whether it is installed, with Download, or Download again and Remove. Typst support is published for Macs with Apple silicon and for Intel Macs."},{"href":"typst.html#start-one","title":"Typst documents › Start one","text":"Choose Blank Typst document as the venue template when you create a project, or New Typst file in the Project panel's + menu. A folder you open that has a main.typ (or another .typ that includes the rest) and no LaTeX document builds that file. In a LaTeX project, a .typ file you build compiles on its own, and the LaTeX paper stays the document other builds compile."},{"href":"typst.html#write-and-build","title":"Typst documents › Write and build","text":"Build and preview: ⌘S, the build button and automatic builds work as they do for LaTeX. Typst also compiles as you type: a moment after you stop, the PDF shows your edit, unsaved, and keeps its page and scroll position. Errors: the build button and the problems card show Typst's errors and warnings with their file and line. Click one to jump to it. Source and PDF: double-click a place in the PDF to put the cursor on that line of its source, even in a file you #include, or press ⌘⇧J to mark the cursor's line in the PDF. Edits you haven't saved count too; build the document once first. Fonts and packages: your system fonts are available, plus the fonts Typst bundles (Libertinus Serif, New Computer Modern, DejaVu Sans Mono). #import \"@preview/…\" downloads the package from Typst Universe the first time; after that it works offline. Overleaf sync and the Visual editor are LaTeX-only for now."},{"href":"workspaces.html#panels","title":"Panels and workspaces › Panels","text":"Everything in the window is a panel: Project, Papers, Agent, PDF and Terminal, and tools such as History, Annotations, Overleaf, TODOs and the Submission checklist. The Panels menu in the title bar shows or hides the first five; the tools open from their title-bar buttons (History, Annotations, Overleaf, Git) or from the command palette (⌘K), for example Manuscript TODOs and Submission checklist. Settings › Appearance › Title bar tools chooses which tool buttons the title bar shows. Drag a panel's tab to dock it anywhere: beside, above or below another panel, or as a tab in its group. ⌘⌥1 Project, ⌘⌥2 Papers, ⌘⌥3 Agent, ⌃` Terminal show or hide those panels. ⌘⇧↩ (Maximize focused panel) maximizes the focused panel and restores the layout again. Focus mode (⌘⇧D) hides everything but the editor. Reset layout in the Panels menu brings back any panel you closed. Under Layout, the Panels menu lists your workspaces and three ready-made layouts: Writing, Reading and Bibliography."},{"href":"workspaces.html#workspaces","title":"Panels and workspaces › Workspaces","text":"A workspace is a saved arrangement of panels. They sit as tabs in the title bar, and ⌘1 to ⌘9 switch between them. Create: click + next to the workspace tabs. The new workspace starts from the layout on screen; type its name and press Return. Save changes: when you rearrange panels, the workspace tab shows a dot. Right-click the tab and choose Save to workspace, or Revert to saved. Rename: double-click the tab, or press F2. Duplicate: makes a copy and lets you name it. Reorder: drag the tab, or use Move left and Move right."},{"href":"workspaces.html#delete-a-workspace","title":"Panels and workspaces › Delete a workspace","text":"Right-click the workspace's tab and choose Delete workspace. If right-clicking doesn't open the menu (some trackpad settings), select the tab and press ⇧F10. The notice that appears (Deleted \"Name\") has Undo, which brings the workspace back. Delete workspace is unavailable while it is the only workspace. When the window is too narrow for tabs, the workspaces fold into one menu with the same actions."},{"href":"writing.html#the-editor","title":"Writing › The editor","text":"A .tex file opens in the source editor, with completion and diagnostics as you type. Above it, switch between: Source: the LaTeX as written. Visual: the same file with headings, emphasis, equations, lists and figures drawn in place. You still edit the same text; switch back any time. The choice applies to every .tex file and is remembered; it is also in the ⋯ menu above the editor. Useful while writing: Format keys: ⌘B bold, ⌘I emphasis, ⌘⇧M inline math, ⌘/ comment a line, ⌘⌥E wrap in an equation, ⌘⇧E wrap in an environment, ⌘⌥I an itemize list, ⌘⌥M math symbols. The full list is in Keyboard shortcuts (⌘?). Find and replace: ⌘F in the file, ⌘⇧F in the whole project. Go to: ⌘P opens any file, ⌘G a line, ⌘⇧O a section or label. Outline: the marks at the right edge of the editor stand for the document's sections; rest the pointer on them for an Outline list with each section's number, title and PDF page, and click one to go there. The PDF toolbar's Outline button lists the same sections. Citations: ⌘⌥K shows the card of the reference whose \\cite key the cursor is on. See Citations and papers. Markdown notes, spreadsheets, HTML pages, slides and boards live in the same project and open in their own editors. Typst documents have a page of their own. Code files: Python, TypeScript, JavaScript, Rust, Go, C and C++, Java, R, Lua, shell scripts, YAML, TOML, JSON, Makefiles, Dockerfiles, CSS, HTML and BibTeX open with syntax highlighting. A fenced code block in a Markdown note is highlighted in the same languages and in the same colors as a file of that language, from the code theme. Markdown tables: drag a column border in the visual editor to resize the column, or double-click it to fit the column to its content. Drag a row's bottom border to make the row taller, or double-click it to give the row its content's height again. Widths and heights are remembered for the next time you open the file, and the .md file itself stays plain Markdown. Spreadsheets: Control-click or right-click opens a cell's menu; ⌘-click adds another range to the selection. Lattice saves as you type. The status bar shows the line and column, whether the file is saved, and the word count."},{"href":"writing.html#select-text-to-work-on-it","title":"Writing › Select text to work on it","text":"Selecting text in the editor shows a small toolbar: Proofread (⌘⌥P), Polish, Rewrite with an instruction and Annotate. The first three ask the Agent for an edit with a reason for each change, which you accept or reject in place; Annotate leaves a note for later. See The Agent. The PDF beside your source, and jumping between the two, are on Building and fixing errors."}];
